Research and compile age-appropriate wake window durations and nap schedules for babies from newborn (0 months) through toddler (24 months).

For each age bracket (ideally monthly or in commonly used ranges like 0-1m, 1-2m, 2-3m, 3-4m, 4-6m, 6-9m, 9-12m, 12-18m, 18-24m), I need:

1. Maximum wake window — how long the baby can stay awake between sleep periods before becoming overtired
2. Number of naps per day
3. Average nap duration (per nap and total daytime sleep)
4. Total night sleep duration
5. Total sleep in 24 hours
6. Recommended bedtime range

Provide a structured table I can use as a reference data source in a mobile app. Include both the ranges (min-max) and a "typical" midpoint value for each metric.

Prioritize evidence-based sources: American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), National Sleep Foundation, peer-reviewed pediatric sleep studies, and widely-cited pediatric sleep specialists (e.g., Weissbluth, Ferber, Mindell). Flag where sources disagree and note the range of recommendations.

Context: This data will drive a baby sleep tracking app that predicts optimal nap times. The app calculates "when should the baby sleep next?" based on age-appropriate wake windows. Accuracy and safety of the recommendations matter — conservative (shorter) wake windows are preferred over aggressive ones when sources conflict.
